nicole buleshenko is 12 years old for  two months she's been sheltering from  the bombs in a city under attack  she is among hundreds of children  surviving underground in harkiv a metro  station now home school  playground and refuge  it is as safe as it gets here but nicole  wants to tell us it is no life at all  uh perez  i can't express how worried i am nicole  tells me it is so painful i'm anxious  about everyone i love  and of the people attacking her city she  says this  they are not human  then i ask about her mother and father  still at home with their three dogs  what about her family are her family  okay at the moment  yes she says  suddenly the things she wants to say  become too difficult but it is a glimpse  of the harrowing impact all this is  having on children here  this is the most important person in  these youngsters underground life  arena zolotoreva is a trauma  psychologist she sent her own children  out of the country because she wants to  be here to help those left behind those  who have seen and heard far too much  all of them went through some things she  says  some of them were caught in the shelling  or the bombing  some of them had no shelter because  their homes got hit  of course each of them has trauma  above ground their world is in ruins  this is what is left of an infant school  nearby  all wars turn children's lives upside  down  but this war is exacting a particularly  cruel cost on the classrooms of ukraine  if the school was a target it is a war  crime  if it was collateral damage it is at  best  dangerously reckless  this is what lessons look like now  in a cramped corner of the metro station  they are working hard here  but towards what kind of future  no one really knows  nicole gets comfort from one-on-one  sessions with arena it helps her  understand more about the catastrophe  engulfing her and despite the strain of  underground life she told me she will  not leave her cave  i've seen and heard everything but i am  not going anywhere i'm going to stay  here till the very end  it is not easy for anyone here  says i just want to comfort them and  make their trauma smaller and smaller  and smaller  these are bewildered and confused  children frightened by what is happening  to their world  a hug goes a long way here because  sometimes there are simply no words
 

